edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172165 #edb/ref 1045 "Sets and Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172165 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172165 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172165 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172165 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186172165 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a4fa77b7-b5dc-582a-b528-7315f5e6da92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172627 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172627 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172627 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172627 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0,3)?$\n\nI. $p:\\{0,3\\}\\to R$\n\nII. $q:R\\to [0,3)$\n\nIII. $r:[0,3)\\to \\{0,3\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172627 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56a15428-b444-550d-95b0-a7a76a54d0a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172627 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[0,3),$ the interval from $0$ to $3$ (including $0$ but not including $3$).\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $\\{0,3\\},$ the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $3$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172630 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172630 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96043" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172630 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172630 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $h(x)=5x+{x}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172630 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2423c376-50d4-5d9b-9b03-1de914522887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172630 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $h(x)=5x+{x}^{2}$ means that the function $h$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $5x+{x}^{2}$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nh:x↦5x+{x}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172893 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172893 #edb/ref 1061 31534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172893 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c0df1c3-781f-5384-8242-e29a5af8b45d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172893 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172894 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172894 #edb/ref 1061 31535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172894 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1cfa3d7e-ad4a-56eb-a574-b1aabd5cdaac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172894 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172895 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172895 #edb/ref 1061 31536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172895 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d0715f6e-ea8f-5320-8fcf-cb71f9bc111a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172895 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172896 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172896 #edb/ref 1061 31537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172896 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "838c5337-7b11-5602-b176-1ada2e0f217d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172896 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172897 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172897 #edb/ref 1061 31538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172897 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "94c9cb06-fe8d-5820-b507-531956879003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172897 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172898 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172898 #edb/ref 1061 31578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172898 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cb125237-85d5-5a53-b57b-8dc014be36e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172898 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172899 #edb/ref 1061 31579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172899 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc522bcd-087e-5658-be5c-e4da5fa77ba5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172899 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173158 #edb/ref 1064 "A function relates an input to an output.\nThe set of all inputs is called the **domain** of the function, while the set of all *possible* outputs is the **codomain** of the function.\n\nTo say \"*the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y,$*\" we can use the following notation:\n\n$$\nf : X \\to Y\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$ and\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nYou might be used to calling the set of outputs of a function as the *range* of the function. However, there is a subtle difference between codomain and range, which we'll learn about during this lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173158 #edb/ref 1065 5041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173158 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6631574e-d0c2-53f4-a74c-af95b5e7ca5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173158 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173159 #edb/ref 1064 "The alternative arrow notation for a function \"$\\mapsto$\" describes to which element of the codomain each element in the domain is mapped.\n\nConsider the following function $f,$ defined by the alternative arrow notation:\n\n$$\nf : x \\mapsto |x|\n$$\n\nThis means that the function $f$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $|x|$ in the codomain. Notice that this can also be written as\n\n$$\nf(x) = |x|.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173159 #edb/ref 1065 5042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173159 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f4f5130f-81b8-5d46-89e9-c016c38883c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173159 #edb/ref 1067 "An Alternative Arrow Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173160 #edb/ref 1064 "How do the codomain and range differ? Recall that in the notation $f : X \\to Y$ the codomain of the function is $Y.$\n\nOn the other hand, the range of $f$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y \\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x \\in X.$\n\nConsider the function $f(x)=|x|$ shown in the mapping diagram below.\n\n![](images/28/28fc79adea3f40c735cc0015069b4dc30433b7a3d372cc00148d6412a8ba27af.png)\n\nInspecting the diagram, we see that:\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X = \\{ -2,-1,1,2,3 \\},$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y = \\{ 0,1,2,3,4 \\},$\n\n- the range of $f$ is $\\{ 1,2,3 \\},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nThe range depends on the domain we choose. If we remove the element $3$ from the domain in the diagram above, then the element $3$ in the codomain will not have any incoming arrows. Therefore, the range of the function will be $\\{ 1,2 \\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173160 #edb/ref 1065 5045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173160 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "65cb7cea-369f-5027-be06-d233d5060975" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173160 #edb/ref 1067 "Codomain vs. Range" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462445 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462446 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462447 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240968 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104954" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240968 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240968 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8f/8ff01afa1993f8e301edb69e3e7a13ee71ab01b1a3da416aa53ee94d021184e1.png)\n\nConsider the function $g:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The domain of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$\n- The codomain of $g$ is ${-1,0,1,2}$\n- The range of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186240968 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86293bdd-1445-5c75-b0fe-3a52f74f9647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240968 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $g:X\\to Y$ means that the function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $g$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X={-1,0,1,2},$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y={0,1,2},$\n\n- the range of $g$ is ${0,1,2},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357810 #edb/ref 1080 "h:\\operatorname{ln}⁡x↦x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357810 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357810 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06831b3b-6061-5239-b68e-1d650bf34e49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357847 #edb/ref 1080 "h(x)=-{x}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357847 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357847 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b1117a3-0b56-51e4-a666-3e78ae949ab1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358323 #edb/ref 1080 "h:x↦\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358323 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358323 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "42e066d9-1f10-53d9-a198-00433dbdd8e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358339 #edb/ref 1080 "h:5x+{x}^{2}↦x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358339 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358339 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4495385b-d4c4-5144-8e9d-c7c73fe54224" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358475 #edb/ref 1080 "h:5x↦x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175